# Cloudflare Durable Objects — API Contract (NON-NEGOTIABLE)

You MUST follow these rules exactly. Violations have caused production-breaking bugs that were invisible due to silent error swallowing. If you are unsure how a Cloudflare API works, stop and read the official Cloudflare documentation. Do not guess, infer, or hallucinate API behavior.

## `DurableObjectNamespace.get()` requires a `DurableObjectId`

**NEVER pass a raw string to `DurableObjectNamespace.get()`.** Always convert first:

```typescript
// CORRECT
const ns = this.env.MyDO as DurableObjectNamespace;
const id = ns.idFromName(name);
const stub = ns.get(id);

// WRONG — this silently fails or targets the wrong DO
const stub = ns.get(name); // string is NOT a DurableObjectId
```

The `@cloudflare/actors` `Actor.get(id: string)` static method handles this internally. The raw `DurableObjectNamespace` from env bindings does NOT.

## Never add permissive type overloads to silence errors

If TypeScript rejects `ns.get(someString)`, the code is wrong — not the types. Do not add `get(id: string)` overloads to `DurableObjectBinding`. Fix the call site to use `idFromName()`.

## Never silently swallow errors

```typescript
// FORBIDDEN in delivery paths
try { ... } catch { return 0; }

// If you catch, propagate or log meaningfully
```

Empty catch blocks in message delivery make bugs invisible. The `toUser()` and `toRoom()` bugs went undetected because errors were silently caught and converted to `0`.

## Test mocks must match the real API

Mock DO bindings MUST include `idFromName`. Tests MUST verify `idFromName` is called with the correct name AND that `.get()` receives the resulting ID — not a raw string.

```typescript
// CORRECT mock
const binding = {
  get: vi.fn(() => stub),
  idFromName: vi.fn((name) => createMockDOId(name)),
};

// WRONG — hides the idFromName requirement
const binding = { get: vi.fn(() => stub) };
```

## No Workers runtime globals in unit tests

`WebSocket.OPEN`, `WebSocket.CLOSED`, etc. are only available inside the workerd runtime. Unit tests running in Node/Bun must define local constants.

## Never use `any`

Use `unknown`, proper generics, or type assertions with specific interfaces. In tests, define a `TestActor` interface to access internal methods instead of casting to `any`.
